{"name":"Enthusiasm","hex":"#00ffaa","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"enthusiasm","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An intensity of feeling; an excited interest or eagerness.","Something in which one is keenly interested."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/enthusiasm"}],"entries":[{"title":"Enthusiasm","blocks":[{"text":"In modern usage, \"enthusiasm\" refers to intense enjoyment, interest, or approval expressed by a person. The term is allegedly related to playfulness, inventiveness, optimism, zest, verve, and high energy.\nPeople can often characterise the modern version of enthusiasm as \"infectious\" or \"contagious\",\nand sometimes even more metaphorically as a virus in its own right."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/EnthusiasticBillyMurray.jpg/330px-EnthusiasticBillyMurray.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enthusiasm","wikidata":"Q840407"},{"title":"Enthusiasm (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Enthusiasm: The Symphony of Donbas is a 1931 sound film directed by Soviet filmmaker Dziga Vertov. The film was the director's first sound film and also the first of the Soviet production company Ukrainfilm. The film's score is considered experimental and avant-garde because of its incorporation of factory, industrial, and other machine sounds; human speech plays only a small role in the film's sounds."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/%D0%9F%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%BA%D0%B0%D1%82_%D0%BA_%D1%84%D0%B8%D0%BB%D1%8C%D0%BC%D1%83_%C2%AB%D0%AD%D0%BD%D1%82%D1%83%D0%B7%D0%B8%D0%B0%D0%B7%D0%BC_%28%D0%A1%D0%B8%D0%BC%D1%84%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B8%D1%8F_%D0%94%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B1%D0%B0%D1%81%D1%81%D0%B0%29%C2%BB.jpg/330px-%D0%9F%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%BA%D0%B0%D1%82_%D0%BA_%D1%84%D0%B8%D0%BB%D1%8C%D0%BC%D1%83_%C2%AB%D0%AD%D0%BD%D1%82%D1%83%D0%B7%D0%B8%D0%B0%D0%B7%D0%BC_%28%D0%A1%D0%B8%D0%BC%D1%84%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B8%D1%8F